g(θ, φ) = ∇_θ f(θ, φ) = 0 where f(θ,φ) = L_S(θ) + λ||θ-φ||². Note λ||θ-φ||² depends on both θ and φ.
Differentiate wrt φ_j: ∂g/∂θ · ∂θ*/∂φ_j + ∂g/∂φ_j = 0 → ∂θ*/∂φ_j = -H^{-1} ∂g/∂φ_j, H = ∂g/∂θ = ∇²_θ f.
∇_φ L_o = Σ_j (∂θ*/∂φ_j)^T ∇_θ L_o = -Σ_j (∂g/∂φ_j)^T H^{-T} ∇_θ L_o. H symmetric ⇒ H^{-T}=H^{-1}. Solve H v = ∇_θ L_o (CG), then ∇_φ L_o = -(∂g/∂φ)^T v. With g = ∇_θ L_S(θ) + 2λ(θ-φ), ∂g/∂φ = -2λ v_... ∂/∂φ [2λ(θ-φ)] = -2λ I (plus if λ is meta-param: ∂g/∂λ = 2(θ-φ)).
So ∇_φ L_o = 2λ v, where v solves H v = ∇_θ* L_o. And ∇_λ L_o = v^T · 2(θ*-φ) ... with sign: ∇_λ L_o = -(∂g/∂λ)^T v = -2(θ*-φ)^T v. Hmm wait sign: ∇_φ_j L_o = -(∂g/∂φ_j)^T v = -(-2λ I)^T v = 2λ v. Yes. And ∇_λ L_o = -(∂g/∂λ)^T v = -2(θ*-φ)^T v.

